--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0120 added a dirty-tree guard before the `git checkout` in `_raid()` (line 966).
A sweep of beat.py found a second unguarded checkout at line 807, in the post-housekeeping
cleanup path. If housekeeping leaves uncommitted files (e.g. a failed partial commit),
this checkout silently fails and the function returns an incorrect outcome.

The pattern: `_git("checkout", default_branch, cwd=path)` without a prior
`git status --porcelain` check.

## Relevant files
- `scripts/beat.py` line 807 — checkout in post-housekeeping cleanup
- `scripts/beat.py` lines 954-964 — the guarded checkout added by 0120 (reference impl)

## Actions

1. In `beat.py` around line 807, add the same `git status --porcelain` pre-check
   that was added in the 0120 fix (lines 958-964):
   ```python
   status = _git("status", "--porcelain", cwd=path)
   if status.stdout.strip():
       dirty_files = status.stdout.strip().splitlines()[:5]
       detail = f"{len(dirty_files)} file(s): {', '.join(dirty_files[:3])}"
       _log(f"=== housekeeping: aborted-dirty-tree: {detail} ===")
       _record_phase_outcome(path, "housekeeping", "aborted-dirty-tree", detail=detail)
       return "aborted-dirty-tree"
   ```
2. Add `"aborted-dirty-tree"` check to the housekeeping outcome handling in the
   supervisor survey if not already covered (check `nightbeat-supervisor-survey.py`
   — `FAILURE_OUTCOMES` was updated by 0120 so this may already be handled).

## Test

Add to `tests/test_beat.py` a test in the housekeeping path that patches `_git` to
return non-empty stdout for `status --porcelain` and asserts the function returns
`"aborted-dirty-tree"` without attempting the checkout.

## Exit criteria

- `beat.py` line 807 has a `git status --porcelain` pre-check before the checkout.
- Dirty working tree in housekeeping returns `"aborted-dirty-tree"`.
- Test covers the new guard.
- `python -m pytest tests/test_beat.py` passes.
